JS: And they-- they were putting small buckets. And one was filled with water, and one was an empty bucket for people to go to the bathroom.

INT: Mhm.

JS: The-- the ride to Auschwitz, I think, was supposed to take several days, from Hungary to Poland. And my mother looked at the bucket, at the buckets, and see, she said to the-- to the commander, in a very nice way, you know, your honor, now, surely you don't want mothers and children, little children, to suffocate on the train, with this-- you know there is not enough water even for one day. I know you're taking us where we're going to die, but you don't want us to die on the train, do you?

INT: Mhm.

JS: And the sergeant who stood there picked his gun up and put it to her head, ready to shoot her. And he yelled at him in German, you dummkopf, don't you realize if you kill her we have nobody to talk to her? So, sure enough, the Germans replaced the small buckets with oil drums filled with water.

INT: Mhm.

JS: And they-- I guess, in that way, half-- maybe the lives of half of the people on the train were saved.

INT: Was this in all the boxcars that it was replaced?

JS: Yeah, yeah. They were replaced in all the boxcars.

INT: All right.

JS: So-- and it-- that was my mother.

INT: Mhm.

JS: You know, she spoke out. She-- she was a risk-taker. And, in this case, it paid.

INT: Do you remember the commandant's name?

JS: No.

INT: OK. Do you remember how long you were on the train?

JS: We were on the train for days-- exact time, I don't remember. I don't remember much more for the train. I don't remember-- I remember getting off the train every four days or so, and we would pull off the dead and pile them up and bury them or burn them or-- mostly bury them, I think.

INT: Mhm.

JS: I was on a train twice, on train rides. So this-- yeah.

INT: All right. At-- the--the first train ride from--

JS: The first train I don't think stopped much. The people were still in good shape there.

INT: Right. And where did you go, from there, on the first--

JS: From there, we went to Austria. I don't know the name of the-- the town we were near, but we ended up on a farm--

INT: Mhm.

JS: --a large, very, very large farm, that was actually used also as a prisoners-of-war place. Because there were a lot of French prisoners of war there. There were some Italian. And the head of the-- the farm, the-- the one who owned the farm-- actually, it was a huge ranch--

INT: Mhm.

JS: --he used to take off his shirt and show us the swastika on his chest. It was the size of the whole chest-- was one big swastika.

INT: Hm.

JS: And I don't remember him personally abusing anybody. Although, from what I heard, he was quite abusive. Because, you know, there were French there, too, and there were Italians. So he couldn't pick on us--

INT: Mhm.

JS: --especially. I have a very strong memory, there. They had an outhouse. And, you know, I was-- I wasn't six yet, and I ran to the outhouse. I had to go to the bathroom.

And I was moving around. And all of a sudden, I find myself inside the hole, holding with my hands. And I was screaming for somebody to come. But somehow, I managed to get out. My brother was coming for me.

And somehow, by the time he arrived, he said, are you OK? I said, what happened? I said, I-- I almost fell in. [LAUGHS] You know? And there we had a-- a-- a period of a few weeks of sheer terror. My mother disappeared.

INT: At the ranch.

JS: Yeah. My mother and my uncle, her brother, used to go out at night and exchange with the farmers. There was a small farm town there.

INT: Mhm.

JS: My mother used to knit children's clothing. No, she went there. She told them, if you give me yarn--

INT: Mhm.

JS: --I'll make clothes for your children. And she was fluent in German. And she was very-- she knew how to-- how should I say-- to be real nice when she talked.

INT: Mhm.

JS: And she started and, before you know it, she was doing it-- working at daytime on the ranch. At night, she was making those yarns-- making children's clothing. And she would bring, I remember, eggs, cheese, milk, bread. Because the farmer fed us very, very little.

INT: Mhm.

JS: After he fed the French and the Germans and the Italians, whatever was left, he gave us. But we were always doing well-- not just us. She-- my mother fed everybody who was on that farm. Because they-- they separated us.

INT: Mhm.

JS: You know, the farm couldn't handle more than so many. One day, she didn't show up. And, about four weeks later, all of a sudden, she-- we thought she was dead for sure, and my uncle. And then she came back.

She'd never wanted to talk about her experience, what happened, but she did tell us that she ended up in jail. How come they didn't shoot you? The orders were shoot on sight when somebody left. When a Jew was caught outside the camp, the order was to shoot on sight.

INT: Mhm.

JS: She said, well, somehow they covered for her a little bit, because the farmers who dealt with her were supposed to be shot, too.

INT: Mhm.

JS: And the sheriff there, the head of the police department, who knew about it, he was going to be shot, too. So there was a deal. And the German officer was a young officer who fell in love with my mother. My mother was very, very pretty.

INT: Mhm.

JS: And very smart. And he was-- he ended up being the only one who got really punished. He was sent to the Russian front.

My mother was in jail. And she was with a-- she told us she was with a German girl, there, a couple of German girls, who were in jail because they would not submit--

INT: Mhm.

JS: --to the orders of the German military to entertain soldiers on furloughs.

INT: Mhm.

JS: They wouldn't go to bed with German soldiers. They ended up in jail, and they were executed.

INT: Hm.

JS: Somehow, my mother got it out. I don't know. Maybe she-- maybe she succumbed, or-- she never spoke about it. She kept her mouth shut, the rest of her life.

INT: Mhm. OK, give me a better idea, please, of what the farm was-- where you were living, how many people might have been there?

JS: The farm was-- had quite a few people, but it was loaded with French prisoners of war. And it was loaded with some Italians. And they were very nice to us, you know, especially the kids.

INT: But were there tents? Were there-- were you in barns? Were you--

JS: I think we had barracks, like--

INT: OK.

JS: --more or less barracks. And I have-- I have a memory, but I don't know what camp it was, that it may have even been at the railroad. No, it couldn't have been at the railroad. One of the camps, I remember, that we were, maybe temporarily, where they wanted to empty the barracks. And these uniformed people who were not German came in. I think they had dark-blue, you know, shirt and pants, and they had a stick.

And I was right next to an elderly fellow I don't know how old he was, but he was old. You know, since I was not quite six, maybe he was--

INT: 25. [LAUGHS]

JS: No, no, he was older than that. He wasn't getting up fast enough, so he just hit him with this steel bar, and-- and killed him right in front of me.

INT: Mhm.

JS: And the-- and I just walked out. It-- it is a memory that it's very possible was one of my nightmares, later on. But I remember it very vividly.

INT: How did you react, at the time?

JS: I ran out.

INT: Mhm.

JS: I was not terrorized. You know, I was never really terrorized. I-- I don't remember ever being afraid, all the time. Because we always did things that-- as little kids.

You know, I had another-- there was another boy about my age that I used to associate with-- with a lot. And I remember approaching a guard who somehow spoke Hungarian, so he may have been Hungarian. And we thought he was friendly to us.

We approached. And all of a sudden, he put the gun and started shooting around us. So then we realized that he was actually-- you know, it was cruel, that what he was doing was actually cruel. We didn't understand why.

INT: So did you have fear, or did you feel protected, or how--

JS: My-- I think it was my mother that kept us so close.

INT: Mhm.

JS: She-- she kind of spread her wings on us-- that I don't remember fear. I remember hunger, to some degree--

INT: Mhm.

JS: --because I was-- I remember doing things that you do only if you are starved to death. You know? But the hunger pangs themselves, I don't. On my sixth birthday-- as I said, we never celebrated--

INT: Mhm.

JS: --birthdays, but then my mother decided to do something and said, you know, you are six today. We're going to have a birthday party for you. And we just got some jelly from the Swiss--

INT: Mhm.

JS: I don't know if the Swiss themselves, but the Swiss organized-- they were the-- the Red Cross, they brought in some packages. It was very unusual that we got some of it, because usually the Germans just--

INT: Mhm.

JS: --looted it-- would give us something. And there was a bowl of jelly that my mother kind of spread it over weeks-- because all we had is bread. So she gave me a teaspoon full of jelly for my birthday. And my brother [? Yakov, ?] who was a year older-- 14 months older-- was always hungry.

INT: Mhm.

JS: Before the war, after the war, he was just always hungry. So I said to [INAUDIBLE], give it to [? Yakov. ?] He's hungry-- or he's hungrier.

INT: Mhm.

JS: That's a memory that I have--

INT: Yeah.

JS: --till today.

INT: When your mother was in the jail, what did you do for food?

JS: I don't remember. All I know is that they-- we were very, very worried. My father was terror-stricken, and so was everybody else.

And then, all of a sudden, she showed up, and it was very joyous. But she never spoke about it. We never asked her. We just asked her what happened. In a very short story, she told us, and--

INT: Mhm.

JS: --she explained why she survived. She didn't know quite the answer why she was not eventually killed.

INT: Right. Was your father working when you were there on the farm?

JS: Everybody was working.

INT: What type of work?

JS: Farm work. He had huge fields. He was growing all kinds of things.

INT: Mhm.

JS: I don't remember vegetables. I remember more wheat and stuff like that.

INT: Mhm. Right-- were you working in the fields, also?

JS: I don't remember.

INT: OK. All right. Do you have any concept of how long you were there?

JS: We were there for quite a while.

INT: Mhm.

JS: It could have been a couple months or so. How and when we moved from there, to Bergen-Belsen, where we ended up, exactly, I don't remember.

INT: OK.

JS: But some transport became available, so they took us. So actually, I spent three times on train rides.

INT: Mhm.

JS: And that train ride, I don't remember at all. My mother says that-- about Austria-- that there were some people who-- who were good.

INT: Mhm. All right. How did you arrive at Bergen-Belsen? What do you remember?

JS: Arriving in Bergen-Belsen, I don't remember. I remember living in Bergen-Belsen, being in Bergen-Belsen.

INT: Mhm?

JS: And, again, I have some very strong memories of a-- a-- I had a furuncle. You know what a furuncle is?

INT: Like a--

JS: An abscess in my head. I still have--

INT: A growth.

JS: --almost a piece missing there. We had a physician, a Jewish physician.

INT: Mhm.

JS: He was not an official physician, because there were no official physicians in the camp. You died, you died. You got sick, you died.

But my mother took it-- took me to him. And he had no-- he had no instruments. He managed to sneak in a scalpel with him, and a couple of things, in his pocket. So he opened me up, and took a dish of pus out of my head.

And he told-- and I remember him saying to my mother, how he survived this, he cannot understand. But I remember-- you know, when he cut me open, there were-- he had no numbing thing. He just cut me. The pain was terrible.

INT: Mhm.

JS: And the pressure-- I had terrible headache, because my head was full with pus.

INT: Yeah.

JS: But that headache disappeared, and I survived.

INT: All right, did you arrive at Bergen-Belsen already with this furuncle. Or did you--

JS: I don't know. I think I probably developed it in Bergen-Belsen.

INT: Mhm. Was it a result of an injury or a hit, or a blow, or anything like that, that you know of?

JS: I don't remember.

INT: OK. All right. Did you receive any medication or anything afterwards for infection? Or-- it just healed by itself.

JS: Nothing. There was nothing.

INT: Yeah.

JS: There was nothing.

INT: Now, when you arrived at Bergen-Belsen, where were you housed? Were you still with your family?

JS: Yeah, we were-- we had barracks. Actually, the-- the pictures you see of bedding, in the movies, is actually where it was-- you know, wooden--

INT: Bunks.

JS: --like-- I think, two or three stories high.

INT: Mhm.

JS: And each one got a block. Each family had one block. And we were concentrated in one block.

INT: Mhm.

JS: In a way, it was good, because kept us a little warmer, being close to each other.

INT: Mhm. Were you allowed to keep your own clothes?

JS: I don't think-- I think we had those-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] those uniforms, you know, the--

INT: Striped uniforms.

JS: --the striped uniform.

INT: OK.

JS: That's why it took me many, many years before I put on anything striped.

INT: OK. Do you remember if you had any of your belongings?

JS: No, we didn't have any belongings.

INT: All right. When you were on the farm--

JS: We had that one little suitcase. We had some-- we had a few of the-- you know, coats and stuff like that, but--

INT: OK.

JS: --yeah.

INT: The-- you had said, when you left the house you had a luggage-- a little bag full of--

JS: That, and we had also-- everybody carried luggage, but how much of it they allowed you to take with you, and once they put you on the trains, I don't remember. Because you were not allowed to take.

INT: All right. And so you arrived with Bergen-Belsen, with what? Do you remember anything that you had?

JS: Basically, whatever we had on.

INT: All right. OK. Now, were you given a job at Bergen-Belsen?

JS: No.

INT: No. What were you doing, every day?

JS: We were loafing, basically doing nothing. We were starving. They fed us once a day-- soup--

INT: Mhm.

JS: --and a loaf of bread. Now, the soup basically was water.

INT: Mhm.

JS: There was some stuff on the bottom. It was potato soup, most of the time--

INT: Mhm.

JS: --sometimes had a little beef in it, but it was all on the bottom. So most people didn't get it.

INT: Mhm.

JS: So it was basically water and some bread.

INT: Mhm.

JS: And usually the bread itself was days old. And that's what we ate.

INT: OK. How was the health of the rest of your family, at this time?

JS: My father always had problems, stomach problems. But basically, the health of the rest of us was, I guess, OK. We survived.

INT: All right.

JS: I mean, we were not-- we were weakened by starvation.

INT: Mhm.

JS: And I was down to-- where-- where I had a head and a stomach.

INT: Mhm.

JS: The rest was nothing. It was shriveled.

INT: Yeah. OK. Do you remember any particular incidents of your time in Bergen-Belsen?

JS: Yeah. The two incidents that-- that struck me most-- one was the-- the one that-- the guard in the tower was talking to us, and my friend and I thought, oh, he's-- he's a buddy, he's a friend, so we approached, and he start shooting at us.

INT: Hm.

JS: He didn't aim to kill us, but he aimed to get us back from the fence.

INT: Mhm.

JS: I mean, I don't see how he could miss. And the other incident that I remember is, my buddy and I, we used to look for German officers that were chewing on things.

INT: Mhm.

JS: So we used to follow them, because we know that they couldn't chew the whole thing. They would throw it. So we were, like, on all fours, like little dogs, following.

INT: Mhm.

JS: And they would throw something, and we would grab it and eat it. And they were eating pork rinds.

INT: Mhm.

JS: And, you know, not everything is chewable on the pork rind.

INT: Yeah.

JS: And one of them must have been new that day, because he-- he asked the other one, oh, what's-- who are this? And he says, oh, just [GERMAN], 'little Jewish doggies,' you know? But we ate! You know? That thing may have-- may have-- maybe that's the thing that sustained me. I don't know.

INT: How did your family react if they knew-- did they know that you were eating pork rinds? Or did they--

JS: I don't think so. But they-- my mother tried to keep us in, but she couldn't. You know, we were little, we were playful. We had to play. So she kept always saying, come back, come back, but she let us out a little bit.

INT: OK. Nobody was working in the family then.

JS: Not in that camp.

INT: All right.

JS: Bergen-Belsen was not a labor camp. It was more a-- a-- a standby camp.

INT: Mhm.

JS: You know, from there, either you were taken for experiment-- experimentation, you were starved to death-- because we were-- that's another thing sticks in my mind-- every day, the funeral hour, where-- where we gathered all the dead, and you had piles of hundreds, every single day--

INT: Mhm.

JS: --piled up. And I remember we-- the-- my father and-- made us say Kaddish. My grandfather made us say Kaddish. And they would take them away--

INT: Mhm.

JS: --to the crematoria, there, to burn them. And that-- it-- it-- it has become so common, that my early memories-- after the camp, in Israel, already, I said-- by the time I was six, I saw more dead people than live people.

INT: Mhm.

JS: So, you know, when-- when I heard about a funeral or I saw-- even today, I look at dead people, I-- I don't get impressed, I don't get scared, I don't--

INT: Mhm.

JS: They are just laying there, you know?

INT: OK. And what were your emotions-- what was your emotional state at the time, do you remember?

JS: I probably was more playful--

INT: Mhm.

JS: --at the time. I was interested in playing. And I found somebody who I could play with, so we played a lot together. And yet I don't remember him at all.

INT: You don't remember his name?

JS: No.

INT: Was he also Hungarian?

JS: Yeah.

INT: OK. Had you known him before the war?

JS: No.

INT: OK.

JS: Not at all. There was another thing, in camp, that somebody made a comment-- and I don't remember if it was my mother or my father or somebody else in the family-- that the camp right next to us, the Hungarian-- we were in the Hungarian camp--

INT: Mhm.

JS: --and the fence-- over the fence were the Dutch. And the Dutch arrived in Bergen-Belsen years before us. And those who were left there, they were actually eating their dead.

INT: Mhm.

JS: Which was not uncommon. Cannibalism-- of course, not cannibalism, but-- you know, you didn't eat somebody who was still alive.

INT: Right.

JS: But when they died, they ate them.

INT: [INAUDIBLE]. OK. [INAUDIBLE].CREW: OK.

INT: OK. Tell me anything else that you recall about Bergen-Belsen.

JS: Yeah. I-- I vaguely remember leaving Bergen-Belsen and why we left Bergen-Belsen. As I said before, my mother was the interpreter. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And my mother was very angry with the kapo, the head Jew of the camp.

She accused him of robbing the food, by skimming the best part of the soup-- the beef, and the good stuff on the bottom-- and giving it to his friends or selling it, even. And she threatened him that she's going to see to it that, after the war, he hangs.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: So, obviously, he got very upset. And she knew that he was planning to do something to her, to kill her. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And he was afraid to do anything right away, because she was the interpreter, and he would have to explain what happened to her.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: And shortly thereafter, the Germans came up-- came to the camp. And again they offered a transport.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: They needed 30,000 volunteers.

INT: Mm.

JS: And my mother, who was always against volunteerism--

INT: Hm.

JS: --never wanted to volunteer-- this time, she did. And she said, I want to get out of here before he does something to me or my family.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: So they loaded us on the train.

INT: Mm-hmm. Who was with you?

JS: We were all together, again.

INT: This is your grandparents?

JS: My grandparents--

INT: Your uncle?

JS: --my uncle, and us.

INT: OK.

JS: And that-- that train was intended to arrive in Hillersleben. We did not know, at the time, the purpose of the ride. But it was already a part of their plan of eliminate witnesses.

INT: Mm.

JS: In Hillersleben, which was perched on the Rhine, at the widest point on the Rhine, the bridge was bombed. So they were just going to shove the entire train into the water.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: [INAUDIBLE] those who managed to arrive there, because people were dying like flies on that train. There was no-- they didn't serve food. And we were on it, god knows, for a couple of weeks or so.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: That was pretty close to the end of the war. And we-- we could tell that the guards on every-- on every car, there was a guard with a machine-gun--

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: --placement. And usually they were teenagers. There were very young. And they were pretty bad, too.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: That train would also stop every so often, and we would take off the dead.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: And one of those dead happened to be an old man who lay next to me, whom-- whom I used as a pillow. It was very convenient. And when we had to take his body off, made me very upset. And in a way was good, though, because we didn't have anymore 75 people in the car. It was not as crowded.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: By the time we arrived, near where we were actually liberated, I'm sure we must have lost 25%, 35%, maybe 40% of the people--

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: --on the train.

INT: OK. All right. The train was on its way to Hillersleben?

JS: Hillersleben.

INT: All right, and what happened to the train?

JS: The night before-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] before we arrived-- of course, we didn't know where we're going.

INT: Right.

JS: So again, we didn't know how long it's going to take or what. We just ran from-- from Bergen-Belsen.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: We heard some bombing in the distance. We were right inside the forest. And it was fairly long, and we-- we heard some airplanes above us, and we were were surprised that we were not bombed. By then, I think, it was a known fact that the planes in the sky were all American planes.

INT: Mm.

JS: And they didn't bomb us. We woke up in the morning. It was total silence all around us. We looked up on the top of the-- you know, we opened the train.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: The train was opened. And we didn't see the guards. They were gone.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: The Germans were all gone. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And all of a sudden, we heard the rumbling of machinery. And sure enough, there was maybe 20 feet, 30 feet to the forest.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: We saw a tank coming out of the woods. And we were afraid--

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: --that here is-- [INAUDIBLE] going to finish us. Sure enough, the turret opened. And I think-- I'm not sure-- the first one to come out of it was black.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: So my father said, Americans.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: Second one was a Jew from Brooklyn. I don't know if the Americans planned it that way, but I have a feeling. Because they say, we know about your train. And they-- everybody jumped on this special Jewish one, that--

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: --I have relatives in Brooklyn. Do you know them? He know everybody.

INT: [LAUGHS]

JS: And also, the-- the tank was filled with candy-- candy bars, and stuff like that.

INT: Did you eat the candy?

JS: Well, I guess we all liked the candy. We were hungry. [INAUDIBLE] I don't remember if we were told not to grab, not to eat too fast--

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: --because we were all very hungry. We were-- [INAUDIBLE] remember the pictures from Rwanda. I think that's what we all looked like.

INT: Mm-hmm. Yeah. OK. But your family managed to survive to liberation.

JS: Yeah.

INT: OK. The tanks came in, and then what happened?

JS: They moved us to Hillersleben.

INT: How?

JS: They-- I don't remember if they-- I think they-- the train.

INT: OK.

JS: They just manned-- the Americans manned the train.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: Or they got Germans to bring us to Hillersleben.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: We lived in a home. From what my mother told me, they-- they-- the Germans-- the Americans evicted Germans from their homes.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: And they gave the homes to Jews.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: And they-- of course, they got immediately involved in trying to fatten us, you know, to-- to feed us.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: Some people, unfortunately, ate too fast.

INT: Right.

JS: And typhus took over. Both my father and my grandfather were-- [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] they got typhus.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: And somehow my grandfather survived, but my father didn't survive. And I remember the day my mother came to us. She was crying.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: Again, I did not feel the enormity of it. You know, she was fighting with the hospital, because he was put in a German hospital, and she wanted him in the American hospital.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: She was afraid that the Germans, those German nurses, were not doing what they were supposed to do.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: You know. And they-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] he died.

INT: Do you know what the date was?

JS: No.

INT: OK. Do you know approximately how long after liberation it was?

JS: Not long after the liberation, because we were taken-- especially the children-- we were picked up by the Americans, by the Joint Distribution, I think. The-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] the Americans-- we were considered-- I remember only six of us.

My brother tells me we had about 150 children, but I have no memory of any other children.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: I think he's exaggerating. Because I roamed the camp. I never saw anybody else but my little friend.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: And they picked the four of us, plus my little friend and his sister, and they took us to Paris. And they made a big deal over us.

INT: OK.

JS: We were considered the first Jewish children to be saved by the Americans.

INT: OK. You were separated from your mother?

JS: Yeah. Yeah. We were separate. Then my mother came to Paris. And-- and she wanted us-- from what I understand, she didn't want to go back to Hungary. The Hungarians asked her to come back, to reactivate the factory. Because, you know--

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: --they lost all those people who knew how to run businesses.

INT: Right.

JS: And she said, no, you shot us in the back once-- no more.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: She didn't want to come to-- to America, either. We had passports. We had family in America.

INT: Hmm.

JS: Says, no more going. And-- but she was willing to take us to Russia. She thought that Russia would be the best place for us, for the four of us. And I think my grandfather told her, you crazy? [INAUDIBLE] finally she decided we were going to Israel.

INT: All right, now, when you went with your brothers and sister to Paris, how were you taken there? And who took you?

JS: I don't remember. I-- I don't remember it was a train or so. I think we were taken by the Americans, the American military, [INAUDIBLE].

INT: How did you react from being separated from your mother?

JS: I don't remember. I remember that-- I vaguely remember it was very tough.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: It was very tough. I didn't want to leave my mother.

INT: Did you think that it was temporary? Did they tell you that she'd be--

JS: Well, my mother kept telling us that it's OK to go.

INT: Uh-huh. All right.

JS: See, my mother was the pivotal person--

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: --in our lives, [INAUDIBLE]-- she was-- I like to-- to describe her as the eagle, you know--

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: --spreading her wings over us.

INT: Yeah. All right, so how long were you in Paris?

JS: In Paris, we were for a few weeks. We stayed at the Grand Hotel.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: And I remember it, because-- first time I ever seen an elevator, and it had benches. So we hid under the bench, ride it up and down. And the elevator guy was very upset. [LAUGHS]

INT: [LAUGHS]

JS: You know, he wanted us out, but we always hid there.

INT: Yeah.

JS: And then we were taken to Orly, in a-- they put us in a pension--

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: --where they healed us. It was a French place.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: We managed in a very short time to learn French. And I spoke fluent French.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: And it was a nice place, very enjoyable. We had a good time there. And then it was time to go. I guess we were waiting for a-- an official transport to Israel. So they moved us thus to Marseille.

INT: Mm-hmm. Tell me, during the time at the farm, the ranch, in Bergen-Belsen, later on the train, did you manage to keep up your religious practices at all?

JS: To the degree, yeah. We had services there.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: My grandparents and my father were all Orthodox people. I don't know if we had any religious instruments with us-- tallis and tefillin.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: I don't think we had any of that stuff.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: But we prayed. My mother always prayed with us.

INT: Mm-hmm. OK. How were your beliefs affected by what you saw?

JS: I was too young. Later on, yeah.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: Later on, growing up in Israel--

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: --as a kid. The camps had a great deal of effect on me. I didn't realize it. You know? I was testing God all the time.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: And I was mad. Why did [? it ?] let it happen?

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: But I was always more mad at our people for letting it happen.

INT: OK. When you arrived in Marseille-- between Orly and Marseille, how did you go? Do you remember how you were transported?

JS: No.

INT: OK, when you stayed in Marseille, where were you staying?

JS: Marseille, they had the-- it was almost like a military barracks, there.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: And we were-- we were comfortable there.

INT: Who else was there with you?

JS: My brothers, my sister, my mother, my grandparents, my uncle. We were all there together.

INT: OK. And how long were you there, approximately?

JS: We must have been there a few months--

INT: OK, and was it--

JS: --two, three months, or--

INT: --was it the Joint that was providing the--

JS: The Joint is-- we arrived in Israel, the four of us, on the Aliyat Hano'ar.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: It's the-- what-- what did they call it? The Youth--

INT: Immigration [INAUDIBLE]--

JS: --Immigration.

INT: Yeah.

JS: And we were put in orphanages in Israel, also belonging to Aliyat Hano'ar.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: That was a part of the Joint Distribution. And they were responsible for clothing us, in Israel--

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: --till-- actually, almost till my military days, in Israel.

INT: OK. All right. Now, when did you leave Marseille to go to Israel?

JS: I don't have the date.

INT: You have the year?

JS: No.

INT: OK. So-- all right. Do you remember how-- did you go on a ship or on a plane or--

JS: On-- we went on a ship, but I don't have any specific memory of the ship.

INT: OK. Do you remember arriving in Israel? Or did you arrive in Palestine?

JS: Well, we did arrive in Palestine, yeah.

INT: OK.

JS: That was before. We arrived there in-- in, I think, '46 sometime-- late '46 or so.

INT: Mm-hmm. OK.

JS: And those were the height of the clashes between-- between the Jews and the-- and the British.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: I remember 'hush.' You know, we mention [INAUDIBLE]-- mention the name of Begin, we did it very silently, very quietly.

INT: Right. Were you legal immigrants--

JS: Yeah.

INT: --to Palestine?

JS: We were legal. Yeah, we were legal--

INT: All right.

JS: --immigrants.

INT: Now, when you arrived, you said, you went to an orphanage.

JS: Yeah.

INT: Can you explain why you went to an orphanage?

JS: Well, my mother-- my father died, and my mother couldn't take care of us.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: So actually they separated us, the three of us. The three boys were put in a yeshiva in Jerusalem. That was my grandfather's choice.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: He was Hasidic, ultra Orthodox.

My mother didn't like it so, a short while later, she pulled us out. And she put us into a-- in a religious orphanage connected to a yeshiva.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: But it was not Hasidic.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: Was [? Litvak. ?] It was--

INT: Right.

JS: --much more modern, where we actually grew up till high school.

INT: Mm-hmm. And where did your sister go?

JS: My sister was put in an orphanage, girls' orphanage, in Petah Tikva.

INT: OK.

JS: And she had a rough time, there, because she was alone. She was separated from everybody.

INT: Mm.

JS: And maybe that was one of the mistakes my mother did after the war.

INT: Yeah.

JS: She should have at least kept her. But she-- she was kind of broken, after the war, you know?

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: She had nothing. [INAUDIBLE] She came from wealth and all, and she arrived in Israel penniless. And she had to work in a factory--

INT: Yeah.

JS: --knitting factory. And I never held it against her.

INT: All right. Now, the three boys were in the orphanage until the army--

JS: Yeah.

INT: --you said. What type of education--

JS: But we were all there-- no, no, we were all there till the age of-- through grade school.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: Grade school, my mother, again, fought for us to go to this finest yeshiva high school in Israel-- Orthodox. Still the very finest, today.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: Graduates end up ministers of government, chief rabbis, and so forth. It was very expensive, but my mother saw to it the government paid for us. She fought for it. My mother as much as went to Ben-Gurion to talk to him, you know.

So finally, they agreed, and we got that education, there.

INT: How often did you get to see your mother?

JS: She worked-- we saw her-- I saw her often because I-- I was a problem. I was a-- they wanted to put me in juvenile-delinquent home. Just about every week, my mother would come and plead that it wasn't my fault-- the camps, and all that. So I saw her a lot. And it-- it broke my heart, you know, to aggravate her, but-- I was very protective of my brothers.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: If somebody touched my brother, it was a death sentence.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: But actually, I was very little, but like a little viper, you know? And--

INT: Do you recall any particular incidents that you can tell us about?

JS: Oh, yeah. I-- I recall incidents of-- of fighting. I used to tell kids twice my size, you want to fight with me, kill me, because I'm going to kill you. So you kill me first.

I recall the-- the counselor, who was a middle-aged man, hitting my brother Yakove. And I jumped on him, from the back, on the shoulders, and I-- he couldn't-- he couldn't dislodge me.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: He left my brother alone. I kept hitting him, for 10, 15 minutes. He couldn't dislodge me. Finally, I jumped off, and I ran away.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: And I ran home, to my mother. And that was quite a distance. It was about 15 kilometers.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: And that was the beginning of the 1948 War of Independence. And there was the Egyptians' airplanes were bombing Tel Aviv for the first time. And here is this plane. I stopped, and I looked, to see the bomb coming out.

And a bus stopped. Everybody jumped off the bus. Somebody grabbed me and threw me under a building.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: When I got home, my grandfather was there. Said, what are you doing here? And I said-- I told him what happened. He says, you better get a bus and go before your mother gets here. Because, you know, there is no excuse in the world that she'll accept.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: Sure enough, she got off the bus. She saw me. What are you doing? I didn't open my mouth. First, she gave me two-- on my face.

She didn't want to listen. She grabbed me. She took the bus, and she took me back.

INT: How did you react to that?

JS: I was mad, but I knew she was right.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: I was mad. I never sassed my mother, never opened a mouth to her.

INT: OK. When you finished high school, yeshiva--

JS: Mm-hmm.

INT: --what did--

JS: 18.

INT: --you do, then?

JS: I joined the-- the military, but I joined it with a group. It's called the [NON-ENGLISH]. Our service was two and a half years, military, and eventually to end up in a kibbutz, form our own kibbutz, along the border, to become a part of the-- of the protection of Israel.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: So we went-- actually, high school-- some of my high-school friends, and were joined by other, and [? we formed, ?] that we were going to spend the rest of our lives together.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: And, indeed, after boot camp, and after some service, we were on the kibbutz. And then, the last six months, we went back to the military.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: And we trained as paratroopers. So the last six months we served as paratroopers. And we trained, every day, day and night.

INT: Was it unusual for Orthodox men, young men, to join the army?

JS: No. Not-- [INAUDIBLE] Ultra Orthodox did not join the army.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: Because they didn't believe in the state of Israel, you know. But the modern Orthodox join the army.

INT: OK. All right. After the paratroopers, do you know when you finished your paratrooper unit time?

JS: It was two and a half years. I was 21 years old, so it had to be 19-- [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] I start 1956 my military service. Sometime in 1959, 1960.

INT: OK. And what did you do after that?

JS: I worked for the government. I managed two villages for the government. I was the administrator of two villages. And my uncle from Canada came to visit, and he offered a little trip--

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: --to come visit me, and I did it. And I-- I ended up in this country. I didn't care for Canada. So I crossed the border on vacation, and I never-- I never went back.

INT: Right. What year did you come to the United States?

JS: '62.

INT: Right. And what did you do when you were here?

JS: I worked for my family in New York. We had a coat factory--

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: --my uncles, my-- my uncles and my cousins. I also attended the Jewish Theological Seminary, there, for a semester. And I met my wife. I married her, and I came to Pittsburgh.

INT: OK. And what did you do for a living here in Pittsburgh?

JS: First, I taught at the Hebrew Institute for four years-- since I was a public-school teacher in Israel.

INT: Hm.

JS: After-- before my military service. Between high school and after teachers' seminary, I became a public-school teacher.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: Then I joined the military when my brother died. And my last job in Israel, actually-- no-- after my military service, I did not go back to teach. I didn't want to teach.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: And I became a government worker.

INT: OK. You mentioned that your brother died. Which brother was--

JS: Yakove, my second-- older brother, who was the same type of military that I was in.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: He was involved in the Sinai campaign. His unit initiated the first attack in Abu Ageila, which is northeastern Sinai. And he [? bought it ?] the first attack-- the-- the defenders of Abu Ageila, the Arabs, they had the tendency of shooting all night long.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: And he got hit.

INT: OK.

JS: So he was the first casualty of the war, of that war.

INT: Do you know when that was?

JS: 1956.

INT: OK. All right, now, when you came to the United States, you said you married. What is your wife's name-- full name?

JS: Barbara Lee Samet.

INT: OK, what was her maiden name?

JS: Schiffman, Barbara Schiffman.

INT: OK. Was she related in any way to your aunt?

JS: No.

INT: That-- OK. You came to Pittsburgh. And you were-- said you were teaching. And what else did you do after that?

JS: Well, I was basically teaching at the Jewish-- at the Hebrew Institute.

INT: Hebrew Institute.

JS: And I was attending Duquesne University--

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: --for approximately two and a half to three years, at which time my mother-in-law was-- got very ill and eventually died. And I joined the family business.

INT: And what was the family business?

JS: Jewelry-- mostly diamonds.

INT: OK. Did you have any children?

JS: I have one daughter, which I always considered to be a miracle baby, because my wife had a terrible time having children.

INT: Mm-hmm.

JS: And, after five years of trying, finally, we had her. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Of course, she was, you know, a miracle of our lives, and we always treated her that way. We gave her best education possible.

INT: What is her name?

JS: Elizabeth Hope

INT: OK--

JS: She's not-- she doesn't care too much about her middle name, but-- [LAUGHS] but she likes her first name.

INT: What-- what's her date of birth?

JS: Her-- she was born August 29, 1968.

INT: And where is she today?

JS: She's in New York. She's a painter, and she's also a graphic artist. And she works for the League of American Theatres and Broadway Producers-- that's Broadway-- as the art director.

INT: OK. I'm going to cut there.INT: OK. You were telling us about your daughter. Can you tell me, did you ever tell your wife or your daughter about your Holocaust experiences?

JS: Very little.

INT: And why is that?

JS: I just couldn't talk about it.

INT: OK.

JS: I get very-- very emotional just talking about it, and, especially when I remember the role that my mother played, that I understand today more than anything, that that was responsible for our survival. Many a times, people ask us, how did you survive? There are no children out there. No children survived. And I said, well, some of it is due to good luck.

INT: Mhm.

JS: But most of it due to my mother.

INT: OK. Tell me what happened to your mother after the war. You said she worked in a knitting factory. And then what happened?

JS: My mother worked in the factory. She kept in touch with us continuously. She established, in fact, an account at a local grocery and candy shop near the orphanage where we were, to make sure that if we needed anything, we just could go there and charge it to her.

I remember that once, after I was disciplined severely, I got mad, so I went to the store and I charged a ton of [LAUGHS] money. I loaded myself up with candy. That was the only time that I have ever done anything against my mother. And I still remember it.

And, shortly thereafter, I don't remember exactly the time, but a few years after we came to Israel, she met this fellow whose name was Moshe Schwartz.

INT: Mhm?

JS: Moshe Schwartz himself was a survivor. Moshe Schwartz was Hungarian whose family moved to Romania--

INT: Mhm.

JS: --for economic reasons, or perhaps social reasons. He was married with three children.

INT: Mhm.

JS: And when the Gestapo arrived in Romania, he-- his wife was taken to Auschwitz, where she died.

INT: Mhm.

JS: The three children-- he paid one of his neighbors to take care of his three children, to hide them. And he himself was taken to a labor camp.

INT: Mhm.

JS: And [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] after the war, he came back looking for his children, and he found out he couldn't find the neighbor. So he kept walking up the street, from door to door. Finally, there was one neighbor who was willing to talk and told him, no sooner were you on the truck that he called-- your neighbor called the Gestapo. And they came and picked up the three children, and they died. He spent the next few weeks looking for his neighbor.

INT: Mhm.

JS: He found him, and he killed them. Then he came to Israel. He was a broken man.

INT: Mhm.

JS: And it came out in his behavior with his son. He had a daughter and a son-- his son Yitzhak, and his daughter Miriam. Miriam lives here in Pittsburgh. She a social worker. Her husband is a dentist.

Yitzhak took care of my mother, as long as she was alive. Yitzhak took the brunt of his father's anger.

INT: Mhm.

JS: He beat him regularly, on a daily basis. He was terribly abused. And this is-- and-- and we couldn't do anything, because we weren't there. My mother tried to stop it, but she was a broken woman, especially after she lost my brother [? Yakov. ?]

And after he died, he took-- he adored my mother, my stepfather. He absolutely adored her. He took care of her. He slaved her-- not that she asked him to. That was his nature.

INT: Yeah.

JS: He had a golden heart. He was a wonderful guy, with one exception-- his behavior with his son. He ruined Yitzhak. Yitzhak managed to graduate the New School in New York, in mathematics. He even got a master's. And after he died, he took over, and he actually took care of my mother full-time.

INT: Mhm.

JS: I helped him financially to be able to do it. I couldn't do it, because I was here and she was in Jerusalem. And after my mother passed away, he got involved with the arts, which-- which he always liked, and became an actor, a tap dancer, a magician, and today he's earning a living, a very good living, in Israel as a magician and as a performer.

INT: Mhm.

JS: My mother knew that she was-- she felt, or she thought she was, a burden on the family, because she was immobile and-- and we never let her feel that way.

INT: Mhm.

JS: We always told her that, why do you feel like that? You are what keeps the family together. You are-- I even told her once, you know, after she-- well, she cried to me, when she was visiting us here, how much burden she is on us.

I said, how can you be a burden on us? You are our national treasure! You are our own Mount Rushmore. You are like one of those faces in the walls.

INT: Mhm.

JS: And in-- in-- in the-- in the last year, I think, after prodding with my brother [INAUDIBLE], we finally sat her down with a camera, like this, and we had their start taping some of her story. She started to talk some about her past. Unfortunately, we didn't get enough out-- out of her.

INT: Mhm. When did she pass away?

JS: It's going to be three years this coming October, I think.

INT: How did you react when she passed away?

JS: It was a little mixed, because she has been-- she had a stroke, and she was in a coma. And I was in Jerusalem. I was going to stay there till the-- till the last moment.

But, when the day before Rosh Hashanah came, I had to come home, as I am on the staff of my synagogue. I'm an unpaid member, but I perform a part of the services. So I came home. And-- and she died Sukkot. I think she died Simchat Torah.

INT: Hm.

JS: And it was a terrible feeling that I couldn't be there. So the next opportunity I had to-- to travel to Israel was the following May.

INT: Mm.

JS: I went with the Jewish National Fund, but my main purpose was to see her tombstone.

INT: Mm.

JS: That was very rough. My sister was here. We sat shiva at her house, because she's orthodox. And-- she was a tough lady. She was-- you know-- you know, we say, there is a whole part in the Bible about the women of valor-- [NON-ENGLISH SPEECH]. You know, means, who can find a woman of valor.

INT: Mhm.

JS: And I don't know who wrote it. I don't know if it was King David or it's from the Bible, but I wonder-- did they ever see Bergen-Belsen?

INT: Mhm. Is there anything that I haven't asked you about today that you would still like to tell us?

JS: It's very hard to-- to gather all those memories, especially since most of it is forgotten. It may be my fault. I just didn't want to look back.

INT: Mhm.

JS: I didn't want to think about it. My big problem was always anger at my own people. Why? If you knew you are going to die, why don't you kill somebody while you did? Why don't you take somebody with you, like Samson did?

INT: Mhm.

JS: Then I was-- I was upset with the-- the American Jews here, who remained silent. They could have done.

INT: Mhm.

JS: They could have, you know, maybe forced politically-- force President Roosevelt to do more. Roosevelt was sitting on information for a year and a half before he did anything.

INT: Mhm.

JS: He had quite a few Jewish friends who could have put the pressure. Frankfurter was the-- what was he, a Chief Justice, or--

INT: Mhm.

JS: He was on the Supreme Court--

INT: I don't know. Yeah.

JS: --was a close friend of Roosevelt. He was asked to intervene with Roosevelt. He says, no, he's doing enough.

INT: Mhm.

JS: So I grew up very mad, till I got to my military days. That's when I first-- I saw what we are, what we are able, and what we are capable of doing. Since the kids, I always-- I lived on those stories of Bar Kokhba, of Judah Maccabee. I mean, our history was fighting. Our history was not giving up.

INT: Mhm.

JS: As much as they tried to explain to me, look, we grew up-- in Europe, we didn't have guns. You know, the-- the Jews were not allowed in the military-- although there were Jews in the Hungarian military.

And then there were acts of heroism here and there, but they were too far and in between. There was an act-- I mean, my stepfather, that-- that-- that's-- I think it's a story that should be told. It's a shame that he's dead. Nobody can interview him anymore. Because he would have never told you the story, anyhow. He never spoke at all about his wartime.

INT: Mhm.

JS: And, seeing how he was beating up my brother, my younger brother, my mother thought that maybe he was a kapo. Maybe that's why he does not talk. Because he was one of those Jews in charge. The Germans knew how to pick him.

INT: Mhm.

JS: You know, maybe some--

INT: Yeah.

JS: --Jews who were criminals before the war. Because they did a-- they did a very, very good job for the Germans.

And here, they were walking in Jerusalem, one evening, along Jaffa Street, and they passed this restaurant. It was very boisterous. They could see that they just got through with either a wedding or a bar mitzvah.

And this group of people came up-- not too many of them. And evidently, they saw my father, my stepfather. And one of them yelled [? 'Moritz?' ?] That's what-- that was his name!

And my father turned around, and he says, yeah, and said, who are you? I said, don't worry. You don't know who we are, but we know who you are. Said, because of you, we are alive.

So, you know, my mother said, what do you mean? So you see, my father was a chef and a baker before the war. So the Germans, when they picked him up and they asked most of the Jews, what do you-- what do you do? What do you know know? I'm-- I'm a carpenter, I'm this and that-- He said, I'm a chef.

So they put him in the kitchen of the German officers. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] And then he would go, every day or every other day, with a truck-- he didn't drive. He sat in the back of the truck. They would go and pick up produce and procurements for the officers' club there. Of course, they got the best of everything.

And as he was coming back with the truck, before he got to the gate, the Jews were working along the fences. He would throw down things from the truck. Nobody looked. He would throw down watermelons. He would throw down bread. He would throw down--

And he said, that's what kept us alive. He said, people were dying-- you know, because they didn't feed them, but they worked them like dogs. He said, that stuff saved our lives.

INT: Is that the same, to you, as shooting a gun or helping or making a difference?

JS: Yeah.

INT: Did that make a difference in your life--

JS: Yeah.

INT: --knowing that?

JS: Yeah.

INT: OK.

JS: Very much. That's why I-- I always had a problem with him-- on one hand, abusing his child, on the other hand, killing the man who-- you know.

INT: Yeah. [INAUDIBLE]. Is there anything else that you wanted to add?

JS: I don't really know. I don't know what to add.

INT: Then I'll thank you very much.

JS: You're welcome.

Yeah, this is really the only picture of my entire family and the only picture of my father that we have left. To tell you exactly when it happened, date-wise, I can't. But, looking at the ages involved here, it must have been in late-- in the late-- perhaps 1939, 1940, or so. On the left is my mother, holding my sister Henja, who's-- as you can see, she is an infant, several months old.

Next to her is [? Moshe, ?] our oldest brother, who's probably approximately maybe five years old here. And then [? Yakov, ?] who is about three and a half, and then myself, around perhaps a year and a half or so, and my father. And that's the only picture of my father that we have left.

CREW: OK.

JS: This is my mother, Rachel. It could have been her graduation picture from high school. She is approximately 17 years old here. As you can see, she's a very pretty woman. And from her dress, she comes from a rather prosperous family.

CREW: OK.

JS: This is my picture, shortly after the liberation. This picture made all the newspapers in France, as we were the first children to be rescued by the Americans. If you look closely, beyond the smile, you see the size of my head. And you can also see a little bit stomach, there. My stomach was blown. And looking at my shoulders, you can tell, just a little bit, under the shirt, that I'm basically skin and bones, with the exception of a big stomach and a big head.

This is the first family picture we took in Israel after the war, perhaps a year or so after the war-- less my father, who died in Germany. And on the left side, you see my second older brother, [? Yakov. ?] Next to him stands [? Moshe, ?] my older brother. I'm sitting there, between my older brother and my mother-- my mother, and my sister Henja.

CREW: OK.

JS: This is a picture of myself with my mother. Seems, from the cup I'm holding my hand, it must have been a festive occasion of some kind. And this is what she looked like till the end of her days. This must have been taken a year or two before she passed away.

CREW: OK.

JS: This is my grandfather Yosef Marmorstein. He was an entrepreneur, and which was-- and it was not uncommon for well-to-do Orthodox Jews to build their own synagogues. He built a synagogue in Debrecen, where he took care of it himself.

He had help, but the name of the synagogue outside needed to be cleaned. So he climbed on a stair and, using lye to clean the sign, it fell on his face. And, as you see, he lost an eye.

He visited-- he visited Israel way before the war-- which was very, very unusual. And he made a business deal in Israel that didn't work out very well. He bought a trainload of oranges. Since there was no air-conditioned trains those days, by the time it arrived in Hungary, it was all spoiled. So he lost his fortune. [LAUGHS] And, of course, next day he was involved in wheat or something else, and he remade it.

CREW: OK.

JS: This is my grandmother on my mother's side, Esther Marmorstein. She came from a very well-to-do family. For old Americans, she did have an American connection. She was the first cousin to George Cukor, who was a major producer in the days of Cecil B. DeMille.

Her name was Esther [? Bloom, ?] before she was married. Her father was a judge and was a major land owner.

This is a picture taken of my military unit. I served in the brigade called, in Hebrew, Nachal, which means 'Pioneer Fighting Youth.' Purpose is to settle the borders with kibbutzim and spend the rest of the life together. Evidently, it didn't work out.

I'm in the-- the very front row, on the right side, two of us, there. I'm on-- I'm the one on the right side.

CREW: OK.

INT: And [? Judah? ?]

JS: This is my second-oldest brother, [? Yakov, ?] who served in a similar unit to mine, preceding me by about two years, and who was the first casualty in the Sinai Campaign, the first attack launched against the Arabs in Abu Ageila, which is in the northeastern part of the Sinai Desert.

Yakov, he was 20 years old. He survived the camps, to die in Israel.

CREW: OK.

